@node Lexical Elements
@section Lexical Elements

The ZIL frontend tokenizer recognizes keywords, operators, literals, and delimiters.

@subsection Keywords

The following keywords are recognized by the tokenizer. Those marked with an asterisk (*)
have special meaning in the parser grammar; others are treated as atoms when encountered
in expressions:

@table @asis
@item @code{<IF>*}
Conditional statement.
@item @code{<ELSE>}
Optional else clause in IF (treated as atom).
@item @code{<THEN>}
Optional then keyword in IF (treated as atom).
@item @code{<WHILE>*}
While loop.
@item @code{<FOR>*}
For loop.
@item @code{<BREAK>}
Break statement (treated as atom).
@item @code{<CONTINUE>}
Continue statement (treated as atom).
@item @code{<RETURN>*}
Return from subroutine.
@item @code{<DEFINE>*}
Define a subroutine.
@item @code{<SET>*}
Assign to a local variable.
@item @code{<SETG>*}
Assign to a global variable.
@item @code{<GET>}
Get object property (treated as atom).
@item @code{<PUT>}
Put object property (treated as atom).
@item @code{<CALL>*}
Call a subroutine.
@item @code{<INVOKE>}
Invoke method on object.
@item @code{<GO>}
Go to label (treated as atom).
@item @code{<EXIT>}
Exit program (treated as atom).
@item @code{<LABEL>}
Define label (treated as atom).
@item @code{<ASSERT>}
Assert condition (treated as atom).
@item @code{<WAIT>}
Wait for condition (treated as atom).
@item @code{<STOP>}
Stop execution (treated as atom).
@item @code{<START>}
Start execution (treated as atom).
@item @code{<FADE>}
Fade effect (treated as atom).
@item @code{<RESTORE>}
Restore game state (treated as atom).
@item @code{<SAVE>}
Save game state (treated as atom).
@item @code{<RESTART>}
Restart game (treated as atom).
@item @code{<VERSION>}
Version directive (treated as atom).
@item @code{<OBJECT>}
Object definition (treated as atom).
@item @code{<PROPERTY>}
Property definition (treated as atom).
@item @code{<GLOBAL>}
Global variable declaration (treated as atom).
@item @code{<LOCAL>}
Local variable declaration (treated as atom).
@item @code{<P>}
Property access (treated as atom).
@item @code{<PRINT>}
Print to console.
@item @code{<INPUT>}
Read input into variable.
@item @code{<DIALOGUE>}
Dialogue/narrative text.
@end table

@subsection Operators

The following operators are recognized and have meaning in expressions:

@table @asis
@item @code{+}
Addition.
@item @code{-}
Subtraction.
@item @code{*}
Multiplication.
@item @code{/}
Division.
@item @code{=}
Equality.
@item @code{<>}
Inequality.
@item @code{<}
Less than.
@item @code{>}
Greater than.
@item @code{<=}
Less than or equal.
@item @code{>=}
Greater than or equal.
@item @code{AND}
Logical conjunction.
@item @code{OR}
Logical disjunction.
@item @code{NOT}
Logical negation.
@end table

@subsection Literal Formats

ZIL supports multiple number literal formats:

@table @asis
@item Decimal
Standard base-10 integers: @code{42}, @code{1024}, @code{0}
@*
@example
(<SET> <VALUE> 42)
@end example

@item Hexadecimal
Base-16 integers prefixed with @code{#x} or @code{0x}: @code{#xFF}, @code{#x10}, @code{0xFF}
@*
@example
(<SET> <COLOR> #xFF)    ;;; Bright white in 8-bit color
@end example

@item Octal
Base-8 integers prefixed with @code{#o} or @code{0o}: @code{#o77}, @code{#o100}, @code{0o77}
@*
@example
(<SET> <PERMISSIONS> #o755)
@end example

@item Binary
Base-2 integers prefixed with @code{#b} or @code{0b}: @code{#b1010}, @code{#b11111111}, @code{0b1010}
@*
@example
(<SET> <FLAGS> #b1010)
@end example

@item Dword
32-bit decimal integers prefixed with @code{#d}: @code{#d65536}, @code{#d2147483647}
@*
@example
(<SET> <LargeValue> #d65536)   ;;; 16-bit maximum value
@end example

@item DWORD String
@code{d'@var{word}'} format: @code{d'SWORD'} (returns string "SWORD")
@*
@example
(<SET> <Token> d'GAME')   ;;; 4-byte identifier
@end example
@end table

@subsection Strings

Text enclosed in double quotes (@code{"..."}). Escape sequences are not supported.

@example
<PRINT "Hello, World!">
<SET MESSAGE "Enter your command:">
@end example

@subsection Atoms

Identifiers consisting of letters, digits, hyphens, underscores, and question marks,
not starting with a digit. Keywords used outside their grammatical context are
treated as atoms.

@example
player-name
get-item-count
action?
@end example

@subsection Delimiters

@table @asis
@item Parentheses
@code{(} and @code{)} group expressions and delimit subroutine argument lists.
@item Comma
@code{,} separates items in lists.
@item Quote
@code{'} denotes an atomic literal (used internally).
@end table

@node Identifier Normalization
@section Identifier Normalization

Identifiers in the ZIL frontend are normalized to uppercase (ZIL is case-insensitive)
when converted to AST nodes. This ensures consistent representation regardless of
the input format.

@subsubsection Normalization Rules

@table @asis
@item Input: @code{myVariable}
@*
Output (AST): @code{MYVARIABLE}

@item Input: @code{MY_FUNCTION}
@*
Output (AST): @code{MY_FUNCTION}

@item Input: @code{GetValue?}
@*
Output (AST): @code{GETVALUE?}

@item Input: @code{local_counter}
@*
Output (AST): @code{LOCAL_COUNTER}

@item Input: @code{Call-Back-Handler}
@*
Output (AST): @code{CALL-BACK-HANDLER}
@end table

All identifiers are converted to uppercase for consistent internal representation.

@node Program Structure
@section Program Structure

A ZIL program consists of object definitions, routine definitions, and statements.
The structure follows the classic Zork implementation pattern.

@subsection Basic Program Structure

@example
<GLOBAL GAME-STATE <>>

<OBJECT ROOM
    (DESC "A generic room")
    (FLAGS LIGHTED)>

<ROUTINE MAIN ()
    <PRINT "Welcome to ZIL!"
    <INPUT LINE>
    <SET LINE <PRIMAIN .LINE>>>
@end example

@node Object Definitions
@section Object Definitions

Objects in ZIL define game entities with properties and relationships.

@menu
* Object Syntax::
* Object Properties::
* Object Relationships::
* Property Values::
@end menu

@node Object Syntax
@subsection Object Syntax

@defsyn
<OBJECT @var{name}
    @var{property1} @var{value1}
    @var{property2} @var{value2}
    @dots{}>
@end defsyn

Defines an object with the specified name and properties.

@subsubsection Examples

@example
<OBJECT LAMP
    (DESC "A brass lamp")
    (SYNONYM LAMP LIGHT)
    (FLAGS LIGHTED TAKEABLE)
    (ACTION LAMP-F)>

<OBJECT SWORD
    (DESC "A sharp sword")
    (SYNONYM SWORD BLADE)
    (FLAGS TAKEABLE WEAPON)
    (SIZE 10)>
@end example

@node Object Properties
@subsection Object Properties

Standard object properties:

@table @asis
@item @code{DESC}
Description string displayed when the object is examined.

@item @code{SYNONYM}
Alternative names for the object (for parsing).

@item @code{FLAGS}
Object flags (LIGHTED, TAKEABLE, SURFACE, etc.).

@item @code{ACTION}
Routine called when the object is the direct object of a verb.

@item @code{CAPACITY}
Maximum number of objects the object can contain.

@item @code{SIZE}
Size of the object (affects container capacity).

@item @code{VALUE}
Point value of the object.
@end table

@subsubsection Examples

@example
<OBJECT CHEST
    (DESC "A wooden treasure chest")
    (SYNONYM CHEST TREASURE)
    (FLAGS CONTAINER LOCKED)
    (CAPACITY 50)
    (SIZE 20)>
@end example

@node Object Relationships
@subsection Object Relationships

Objects form a hierarchy through container and location properties:

@table @asis
@item @code{CONTAINER}
List of objects contained within this object.

@item @code{LOC}
Location of the object (the object that contains it).
@end table

@subsubsection Examples

@example
<OBJECT ROOM1
    (DESC "The entrance hall")
    (FLAGS LIGHTED>
    <OBJECT LAMP (LOC ROOM1) (DESC "A brass lamp")>
    <OBJECT KEY (LOC ROOM1) (DESC "A small key")>>

<OBJECT PLAYER
    (DESC "You")
    (FLAGS TRYTAKE>
    <OBJECT SWORD (LOC PLAYER) (DESC "A sharp sword")>
@end example

@node Property Values
@subsection Property Values

Property values can be:

@table @asis
@item Numbers
@code{(SIZE 10)}

@item Strings
@code{(DESC "A brass lamp")}

@item Atoms (flags/synonyms)
@code{(FLAGS LIGHTED TAKEABLE)}

@item Object references
@code{(LOC ROOM1)}

@item Routine references
@code{(ACTION LAMP-F)}
@end table

@node Routine Definitions
@section Routine Definitions

Routines (functions/subroutines) perform computations and game logic.

@defsyn
<DEFINE @var{name} (<@var{param1} @var{param2} @dots{}>)
    @var{body}>
@end defsyn

@defsyn
<ROUTINE @var{name} (@var{param1} @var{param2} @dots{})
    @var{body}>
@end defsyn

Defines a routine with optional parameters and body.

@subsubsection Parameters

@table @asis
@item @var{name}
Routine identifier.
@item @var{paramN}
Parameter names (may be empty for nullary routines).
@item @var{body}
ZIL statements forming the routine body.
@end table

@subsubsection Examples

@example
<DEFINE INITIALIZE-GAME ()
    <SET SCORE 0>
    <SET MOVES 0>
    <SET CURRENT-ROOM START-ROOM>>

<DEFINE ADD-SCORE (POINTS)
    <SET SCORE <+ .SCORE .POINTS>>>

<ROUTINE LOOK-AROUND (WHICH-ROOM)
    <PRINT <GETP .WHICH-ROOM ,P-DESC>>
    <PRINT "You can see:">
    <PRINT <GETP .WHICH-ROOM ,P-CONTENTS>>>
@end example

@subsubsection Routine Return

Routines return values through the <RETURN> statement:

@example
<DEFINE GET-SCORE ()
    <RETURN .SCORE>>

<DEFINE CALCULATE-BONUS (LEVEL TIME)
    <RETURN <* .LEVEL <* .TIME 10>>>
@end example

@node Syntax Definitions
@section Syntax Definitions

ZIL syntax definitions map player input to game actions.

@defsyn
<SYNTAX @var{verb} = @var{routine>}
@end defsyn

@defsyn
<SYNTAX @var{verb} @var{direct-object} = @var{routine>>
@end defsyn

@defsyn
<SYNTAX @var{verb} @var{direct-object} @var{preposition} @var{indirect-object} = @var{routine>>
@end defsyn

Defines how a verb parses player input and maps it to a routine.

@subsubsection Parameters

@table @asis
@item @var{verb}
The verb name (e.g., TAKE, DROP, EXAMINE).
@item @var{direct-object}
Object pattern for the direct object.
@item @var{preposition}
Preposition (e.g., WITH, IN, ON).
@item @var{indirect-object}
Object pattern for the indirect object.
@item @var{routine}
Routine to execute when syntax matches.
@end table

@subsubsection Examples

@example
<SYNTAX TAKE = TELLER>
<SYNTAX TAKE OBJECT = TAKE-IT>
<SYNTAX DROP OBJECT = DROP-IT>
<SYNTAX PUT OBJECT IN OBJECT = PUT-IT>
<SYNTAX OPEN OBJECT WITH OBJECT = OPEN-IT>
@end example

@subsubsection Object Patterns

Object patterns specify what objects match:

@table @asis
@item @code{OBJECT}
Any game object.
@item @code{GLOBAL}
Objects in global scope.
@item Specific objects
Named objects: @code{LAMP}, @code{SWORD}
@end table

@node Statements
@section Statements

@menu
* Assignment Statements::
* Subroutine Call::
* Return Statement::
* Print Statement::
* Input Statement::
* Dialogue Statement::
@end menu

@node Assignment Statements
@subsection Assignment Statements

@subsubsection Local Variable Assignment

@defsyn
<SET @var{variable} @var{value}>
@end defsyn

Assigns a value to a local variable.

@subsubsection Examples

@example
<SET SCORE 0>
<SET COUNTER <+ .COUNTER 1>>
<SET NAME "Player">
@end example

@subsubsection Global Variable Assignment

@defsyn
<SETG @var{variable} @var{value}>
@end defsyn

Assigns a value to a global variable.

@subsubsection Examples

@example
<SETG GAME-STATE <>>
<SETG HIGH-SCORE 1000>
@end example

@node Subroutine Call
@subsection Subroutine Call

@defsyn
<CALL @var{routine-name}>
@end defsyn

@defsyn
<CALL @var{routine-name} @var{arg1} @var{arg2} @dots{}>
@end defsyn

Invokes a subroutine. Arguments are passed by value.

@subsubsection Examples

@example
<CALL INITIALIZE-GAME>
<CALL ADD-SCORE 100>
<CALL LOOK-AROUND .CURRENT-ROOM>
@end example

@subsubsection Method Invocation

@defsyn
<INVOKE @var{object} @var{method}>
@end defsyn

Invokes a method on an object.

@subsubsection Examples

@example
<INVOKE PLAYER UPDATE>
<INVOKE LAMP DESCRIBE>
@end example

@node Return Statement
@subsection Return Statement

@defsyn
<RETURN>
@end defsyn

@defsyn
<RETURN @var{value}>
@end defsyn

Returns from the current subroutine. Optional value is the return value.

@subsubsection Examples

@example
<RETURN>
<RETURN 0>
<RETURN .SCORE>
@RETURN <+ .A .B>>
@end example

@node Print Statement
@subsection Print Statement

@defsyn
<PRINT @var{string}>
@end defsyn

@defsyn
<PRINT @var{expr1} @var{expr2} @dots{}>
@end defsyn

Outputs text to the console. Multiple expressions are concatenated.

@subsubsection Examples

@example
<PRINT "Hello, World!">
<PRINT "Score: " .SCORE>
<PRINT "You are in " <GETP .ROOM ,P-DESC>>>
@end example

@node Input Statement
@subsection Input Statement

@defsyn
<INPUT @var{variable}>
@end defsyn

Reads a line of input from the player and stores it in the variable.

@subsubsection Examples

@example
<INPUT LINE>
<INPUT COMMAND>
@end example

@subsubsection Input Types

@itemize @bullet
@item For text adventures, input is typically stored as a string
@item The <PRIMAIN> function can parse input into parsed structures
@end itemize

@node Dialogue Statement
@subsection Dialogue Statement

@defsyn
<DIALOGUE @var{text}>
@end defsyn

Displays narrative or dialogue text (synonym for PRINT with special semantics).

@subsubsection Examples

@example
<DIALOGUE "You enter a dark room.">
<DIALOGUE "The door creaks ominously.">
@end example

@node Control Flow
@section Control Flow

@menu
* Conditional Statement::
* While Loop::
* For Loop::
* Break and Continue::
@end menu

@node Conditional Statement
@subsection Conditional Statement: <IF>

@defsyn
<IF @var{condition}
    @var{then-form}>
@end defsyn

@defsyn
<IF @var{condition}
    @var{then-form}
    @var{else-form}>
@end defsyn

Executes @var{then-form} if condition is true (non-zero), otherwise executes
@var{else-form} if present.

@subsubsection Syntax Notes

The THEN and ELSE keywords are optional and serve only as visual markers;
the structure is determined by position. The condition is an expression that
evaluates to true (non-zero) or false (zero).

@subsubsection Examples

@example
<IF <G? .SCORE 100>
    <PRINT "You scored 100 points!">>

<IF <L? .HEALTH 10>
    <PRINT "You're almost dead!">
    <SET GAME-OVER T>>
@end example

@node While Loop
@subsection While Loop

@defsyn
<WHILE @var{condition}
    @var{body}>
@end defsyn

Repeats body while condition remains true.

@subsubsection Examples

@example
<SET I 0>
<WHILE <L? .I 10>
    <PRINT "Count: " .I>
    <SET I <+ .I 1>>>

<WHILE <NOT <EQUAL? .COMMAND "QUIT">>
    <CALL PROCESS-INPUT>
    <CALL UPDATE-GAME>>
@end example

@node For Loop
@subsection For Loop

@defsyn
<FOR @var{variable} @var{start} @var{end}
    @var{body}>
@end defsyn

Repeats body with variable iterating from start to end.

@subsubsection Examples

@example
<FOR I 1 10
    <PRINT "Number: " .I>>

<FOR J 0 <- .COUNT 1>
    <SET ITEM <GET .ITEMS .J>>
    <CALL PROCESS-ITEM .ITEM>>
@end example

@subsubsection Notes

The variable is initialized to start, incremented by 1 each iteration, and the
loop continues while the variable is less than or equal to end.

@node Break and Continue
@subsection Break and Continue

@subsubsection Break

@defsyn
<BREAK>
@end defsyn

Exits the innermost enclosing loop immediately.

@subsubsection Examples

@example
<SET I 0>
<WHILE <L? .I 100>
    <IF <EQUAL? <GET .ITEMS .I> .TARGET>
        <PRINT "Found at index: " .I>
        <BREAK>>
    <SET I <+ .I 1>>>
@end example

@subsubsection Continue

@defsyn
<CONTINUE>
@end defsyn

Skips the remainder of the current loop iteration.

@subsubsection Examples

@example
<SET I 0>
<WHILE <L? .I 100>
    <SET VALUE <GET .ITEMS .I>>
    <IF <L? .VALUE 0>
        <SET I <+ .I 1>>
        <CONTINUE>>
    ; Process non-negative values
    <SET I <+ .I 1>>>
@end example

@node Property Access
@section Property Access

Objects have properties that can be read and modified.

@menu
* Get Property::
* Put Property::
* Property Numbers::
@end menu

@node Get Property
@subsection Get Property

@defsyn
<GET @var{object} @var{property}>
@end defsyn

Retrieves the value of a property from an object.

@subsubsection Examples

@example
<GETP .LAMP ,P-DESC>
<GET .INVENTORY .INDEX>
<SET DESC <GETP .ROOM ,P-DESC>>
@end example

@node Put Property
@subsection Put Property

@defsyn
<PUT @var{object} @var{property} @var{value}>
@end defsyn

Sets the value of a property on an object.

@subsubsection Examples

@example
<PUT .LAMP ,P-DESC "A shiny new lamp">
<PUT .PLAYER ,P-CAPACITY 10>
@end example

@node Property Numbers
@subsection Property Numbers

Standard property numbers used with GETP and PUTP:

@table @asis
@item @code{,P-DESC}
Object description.
@item @code{,P-SYNONYM}
Synonym list.
@item @code{,P-FLAGS}
Object flags.
@item @code{,P-LOC}
Object location.
@item @code{,P-CONTENTS}
Object contents.
@item @code{,P-ACTION}
Action routine.
@item @code{,P-CAPACITY}
Container capacity.
@item @code{,P-SIZE}
Object size.
@item @code{,P-VALUE}
Point value.
@end table

@node Game Commands
@section Game Commands

The following commands are available for game control:

@table @asis
@item @code{<EXIT>}
Exit the program. Optional exit code.
@*
@example
<EXIT>
<EXIT 1>
@end example

@item @code{<WAIT>}
Wait/delay. Optional duration.
@*
@example
<WAIT>
<WAIT 100>
@end example

@item @code{<STOP>}
Stop execution.
@*
@example
<STOP>
@end example

@item @code{<ASSERT>}
Assert condition is true (for debugging).
@*
@example
<ASSERT <G? .HEALTH 0> "Health should be non-negative">
@end example
@end table

@node Input and Output
@section Input and Output

The ZIL frontend provides I/O commands for user interaction:

@table @asis
@item @code{<PRINT @var{text}>}
Display text (see @ref{Print Statement})

@item @code{<INPUT @var{var}>}
Read input (see @ref{Input Statement})

@item @code{<DIALOGUE @var{text}>}
Display narrative text (see @ref{Dialogue Statement})
@end table

@node Unsupported Features
@section Unsupported Features

The ZIL frontend does not implement the full ZIL language. Features such as:

@table @asis
@item Object manipulation beyond basic properties
<OBJECT>, <PROPERTY>, <P> have limited implementation.

@item Goto and labels
<GO>, <LABEL> are recognized as tokens but lack full grammatical support.

@item System calls
<GET>, <PUT>, <WAIT> have limited implementation.

@item Preprocessor directives
<DEFINE> for macros and conditional compilation is limited.

@item Advanced parser integration
The full Zork parser (PRIMAIN, SYNTAX, etc.) requires runtime support.
@end table

These features are recognized as tokens but may lack complete grammatical definition
in this frontend. They may be used as atoms in expressions or processed by external
tools preceding the Eightbol compiler.

@node Example Programs
@section Example Programs

@subsubsection Adventure Game Room

@example
<GLOBAL GAME-STATE <>>

<OBJECT START-ROOM
    (DESC "The Entrance Hall")
    (SYNONYM HALL ROOM)
    (FLAGS LIGHTED)
    (CAPACITY 100)
    <OBJECT LAMP
        (DESC "A brass lamp")
        (SYNONYM LAMP LIGHT)
        (FLAGS TAKEABLE LIGHTED)
        (SIZE 5)
        (ACTION LAMP-F)>
    <OBJECT KEY
        (DESC "A small brass key")
        (SYNONYM KEY)
        (FLAGS TAKEABLE)>
    <OBJECT DOOR
        (DESC "A heavy oak door")
        (SYNONYM DOOR)
        (FLAGS DOOR LOCKED)>>

<ROUTINE LAMP-F ()
    <PRINT "The lamp glows warmly.">>

<ROUTINE MAIN ()
    <PRINT "Welcome to ZIL!"
    <CALL LOOK-AROUND START-ROOM>
    <INPUT COMMAND>
    <PRINT "You said: " .COMMAND>>
@end example

@subsubsection Inventory System

@example
<GLOBAL PLAYER-INVENTORY <>>

<OBJECT PLAYER
    (DESC "Yourself")
    (FLAGS NOALL)>

<DEFINE ADD-TO-INVENTORY (ITEM)
    <SETG PLAYER-INVENTORY <INSERT .ITEM .PLAYER-INVENTORY>>
    <PRINT "Taken.">>

<DEFINE REMOVE-FROM-INVENTORY (ITEM)
    <SETG PLAYER-INVENTORY <REMOVE .ITEM .PLAYER-INVENTORY>>
    <PRINT "Dropped.">>

<DEFINE SHOW-INVENTORY ()
    <PRINT "You are carrying:">
    <COND
        (<NULL? .PLAYER-INVENTORY>
            <PRINT "  Nothing.">)
        (T
            <FOR I 1 <LENGTH .PLAYER-INVENTORY>
                <PRINT "  " <GET .PLAYER-INVENTORY <* .I 2>>>>>
@end example

@subsubsection Command Parser

@example
<DEFINE PARSE-COMMAND (INPUT)
    <SET VERB <GET .INPUT 0>>
    <SET OBJECT <GET .INPUT 1>>
    <COND
        (<EQUAL? .VERB 'TAKE>
            <CALL DO-TAKE .OBJECT>)
        (<EQUAL? .VERB 'DROP>
            <CALL DO-DROP .OBJECT>)
        (<EQUAL? .VERB 'EXAMINE>
            <CALL DO-EXAMINE .OBJECT>)
        (<EQUAL? .VERB 'INVENTORY>
            <CALL SHOW-INVENTORY>)
        (T
            <PRINT "I don't understand.">)>>

<DEFINE DO-TAKE (OBJECT)
    <COND
        (<IN? .OBJECT ,HERE>
            <ADD-TO-INVENTORY .OBJECT>)
        (T
            <PRINT "You don't see that here.">)>>
@end example
